\description{
Low-level sampler for binary quantile regression with an adaptive-lasso
penalty, in which each slope receives its own shrinkage parameter. Most
users should call \code{\link[=bbqr]{bbqr()}} with \code{penalty = "alasso"} instead; this function
is exported for direct access to the sampler.
With the defaults -- \code{model = "v5"} on the prior, \code{anchor = "sigma1"}, no
clamps -- every transition implements the corresponding full conditional
in the V5 appendix: the ALD inverse-scale is held at \code{sigma_fixed}, the
\code{omega} draw is \verb{Gamma(alpha_omega + k delta, beta_omega + sum(1 / lambdasq))}, the intercept carries its \eqn{\tau}-calibrated normal prior,
and \code{delta} is updated by a random-walk Metropolis step on the log scale
under a proper \code{Gamma(alpha_delta, beta_delta)} prior. That last prior is
required rather than optional: see \code{\link[=prior]{prior()}}. \code{anchor = "free"} samples
the inverse-scale from its full conditional instead, which the appendix
also covers.
}
\section{Derivation version}{

Which of the three adaptive-lasso hierarchies is sampled is set by
\code{model} on the \code{\link[=prior]{prior()}} object, not by an argument here. \code{"v4"} and \code{"v5"}
are derivation-faithful: their appendices state that no clamp, no
sampler-argument floor, and no \code{exp(-kappa_lam / lambda_j^2)} tilt is part
of the target, so a fit that names them while enabling one of those devices
is refused rather than recorded. Concretely, under \code{model = "v4"} or
\code{"v5"} this function requires \code{clip = "none"} (the default), \code{kappa_lam = 0}
and \code{sampler = "gig_half"}. \code{model = "v3"} leaves all three switches
